{"type": "FeatureCollection", "metadata": {"note": "The Southern Railway's New York-New Orleans flagship and its predecessors, the direct historical ancestor of Amtrak's Crescent. Southern was one of the few railroads that declined to join Amtrak in 1971, running this train itself until 1979. Route geometry is taken from the modern Amtrak route's own GTFS-sourced alignment for this corridor (see amtrak.geojson), since the historical train ran the same physical line; any place where the historical routing differed is described in the segment notes and reflected in the geometry. Era boundaries follow real, sourced milestones (train introductions, renamings, mergers, discontinuation dates); headway_min and avg_speed_kmh within each era are estimates, not from real timetables.", "source": "Wikipedia (Crescent - Southern Railway train), american-rails.com, southern.railfan.net", "pulled": "20260817", "line_label_audit": "2026-08-24: line labels normalised repo-wide to \"<operating company>: <line name>\" -- the company's common display name (legal suffixes dropped), then the line as a passenger would have known it, or its two terminals where the line carried no advertised name. Long intermediate station lists were reduced to endpoints. For features spanning several operators the company shown is the one that ran the line for most of its drawn years. See CLAUDE.md Rule 6."}, "features": [{"type": "Feature", "properties": {"company": "Southern Railway", "line": "Southern Railway: Crescent", "id": "southern-crescent", "color": "#006747", "mode": "intercity_rail", "avg_speed_kmh": 70, "speed_source": "estimated/blended across the line's history; unsourced", "segments": [{"start": 1891, "end": 1925, "headway_min": 1440, "note": "Lineage begins with the Washington & Southwestern Vestibuled Limited, inaugurated 1891 by Southern predecessor the Richmond & Danville. estimated; frequency not sourced from a real timetable", "sources": ["Segment-level: this segment's own note carries the citation for its dates and service. Supporting references for the line:", "Wikipedia (Crescent - Southern Railway train), american-rails.com, southern.railfan.net"]}, {"start": 1925, "end": 1934, "headway_min": 1440, "note": "Relaunched Apr 26 1925 as the Crescent Limited, a deluxe extra-fare all-Pullman train running New Orleans-New York over four railroads: the L&N, the West Point Route, the Southern, and the Pennsylvania. estimated; frequency not sourced from a real timetable", "sources": ["Segment-level: this segment's own note carries the citation for its dates and service. Supporting references for the line:", "Wikipedia (Crescent - Southern Railway train), american-rails.com, southern.railfan.net"]}, {"start": 1934, "end": 1939, "headway_min": 1440, "note": "The Crescent Limited name, extra fare, and apple-green livery were dropped during the Depression, in 1934. estimated; frequency not sourced from a real timetable", "sources": ["Segment-level: this segment's own note carries the citation for its dates and service. Supporting references for the line:", "Wikipedia (Crescent - Southern Railway train), american-rails.com, southern.railfan.net"]}, {"start": 1939, "end": 1970, "headway_min": 1440, "note": "Renamed simply the Crescent in 1939, gaining the Southern's first air-conditioned coach equipment. estimated; frequency not sourced from a real timetable", "sources": ["Segment-level: this segment's own note carries the citation for its dates and service. Supporting references for the line:", "Wikipedia (Crescent - Southern Railway train), american-rails.com, southern.railfan.net"]}, {"start": 1970, "end": 1979, "headway_min": 1440, "note": "Southern combined the Southerner with the Crescent in 1970 to form the Southern Crescent, running Boston/New York-New Orleans in conjunction with Penn Central. Southern stayed out of Amtrak in 1971 and ran this itself until exiting the passenger business in 1979, when Amtrak took the train over - see amtrak.geojson, 